          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "pipelined_m still yields 1 result/cycle (same throughput as pipelined) but registers only every m stages, cutting FF count and area at the cost of a longer combinational path (lower Fmax). Since the objective is min area and the accuracy/throughput constraints are identical to pipelined, this family trades area against Fmax and is the natural second point on the Pareto front. m in 2..4 keeps the combinational depth bounded so Fmax can still clear the 32 Msps / 0.5 us batch bound, while reducing register area versus m=1. Same data_width/n_iter/guard/rounding ranges as pipelined so the two fronts are directly comparable and merge cleanly."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is a min-area problem with two hard constraints: throughput_msps >= 32 and sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 (32 back-to-back results in 0.5 us), plus max_abs_err <= 2^-12. Only the 1-result/cycle families (pipelined, pipelined_m) can plausibly hit 32 Msps without an unrealistically high Fmax, so I restrict to those two. Accuracy is the binding accuracy constraint: with Q1.(W-2) output the LSB is 2^-(W-2), so W=14 gives a 12-bit LSB and W=16 a 14-bit LSB; I bound data_width to 14..18 to cover the accuracy requirement without oversizing. n_iter 12..16 is the sweet spot where max_abs_err sits near/below 2^-12 while the per-stage path stays short enough for a high Fmax (needed for both the 32 Msps throughput and the 0.5 us batch bound). angle_guard 0..2 and frac_guard 0..2 keep the datapath narrow (min area) while giving enough headroom for the error budget; rounding is left to both choices so the search can find the cheaper trunc vs round trade-off. I split the budget evenly: pipelined (m=1) is the low-area/low-FF anchor and pipelined_m (m=2..4) is the area-reduced variant, together spanning the area-vs-Fmax trade-off that defines the front. This keeps the search focused on the feasible region rather than wasting evals on wide/low-N corners that fail accuracy or on narrow/high-N corners that fail Fmax."
}

